\par
DETAILED COMMENTS (to be returned to the author(s))\par
[Reviewers are encouraged to provide substantitive comments \par
supporting their numerical scale ratings. Reviews without \par
such comments may not be considered in the overall rating of the paper.]\par

This paper shows that when an image set is partially annotated with
keywords, multimodality searches achieve higher accuracy than can be 
obtained by using visual information alone. Although the paper is 
fairly well written, the authors have not proposed any new techniques 
or presented any results that are not already known.  In the CBIR 
community, it is well known that multimodality can improve image-search 
accuracy.  Merely confirming this fact does not warrant the publication 
of this work.

The paper would have been more interesting had the authors proposed 
reliable methods to provide annotation to images, or had they provided 
in-depth treatment on, for example, the following subjects:

o Textual features. The paper would benefit by detailing how exactly 
Wordnet was used by the system.  Wordnet may be able to link "ocean"  to 
"sea" but it may fail in many other situations, such as "International 
waters".  The authors should also explain the limitations of Wordnet. 
(For instance, a very general word such as "water" may retrieve too many 
images.)   When Wordnet is counter-productive, could the effectiveness 
of the tunnel technique suffer as well?

o Image features.  The authors use color features only, even though
they seem to acknowledge that texture and shape features would help.
With rather weak visual features, the performance gap between
visual searches and multimodality searches is certainly large.
When visual features are more robust, I expect the gap in Figure 6 
to be substantially smaller. Has the effectiveness of "tunneling" 
become exaggerated because of the poor visual features?

o Ad hoc indexing.  It is believable that annotating 5% selected
images can work better than randomly annotate 40% of the images.
However, the procedure of selecting the most useful images
to annotate should be precisely documented.  The authors refer to 
using clustering to assist in grouping semantically- and 
visually-related images.  Unfortunately, obtaining quality 
clusters is still an open research problem when an image dataset 
is very large and when the data dimension is very high.  When the 
cluster quality is below par, would tunneling be less effective?
